   "l": "Look at it and do not see it — its name is called the Featureless;\nlisten for it and do not hear it — its name is called the Faint;\ngrasp at it and do not get it — its name is called the Fine.\nThese three\ncannot be pressed with questions,\nand so they blend and become one.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "You look and do not see it: its name is the featureless. You listen and do not hear it: its name is the faint. You reach and do not grasp it: its name is the fine. These three will not be run down by questioning, and so they merge into one.",
   "n": "名曰 is 'the name is called': 曰 is the ordinary verb 'to say', used to open what amounts to quotation marks around a name. All three names are ordinary words pressed into service — 夷 'level, unmarked', 希 'thin, rare', 微 'minute' — which is why no two English versions of this passage agree. Be careful whose gloss you are reading. The neat one-to-one keys usually quoted with this chapter — no colour, no sound, no shape — are the Heshanggong commentary's, not Wang Bi's. Wang Bi does not parcel them out one by one; his comment says only 無狀無象，無聲無響, 'no shape and no image, no sound and no echo', and that the three names are taken from what our ears, eyes and hands fail to do, which is also why the next line says they cannot be pressed with questions.",
   "p": true
  },
