   "l": "Its upper side is not bright,\nits under side is not dark.\nOn and on, past naming,\nit turns back home to no-thing.\nThis is called the shape of no shape,\nthe image of no thing,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Above it, no glare; below it, no gloom. Unbroken, beyond any naming, it goes back again to nothing at all. This is called a shape with no shape to it, an image with nothing in it —",
   "n": "繩繩 is a doubled word: 繩 alone is a cord, and doubled it means going on and on unbroken, like rope paid out. Chinese doubles words constantly to give a texture rather than a count — you met 緜緜 in chapter 6. 復歸於 'return back to' is one of the book's fixed motions and will recur; 於 marks the place reached. The last two phrases run the same frame twice, 無 A 之 B: 'the 狀 of no-狀', 'the 象 of no-物'. In the first the two slots hold the same character, which is where the paradox bites; in the second they do not, and the phrase merely says what it says. Either way the shape of the thought is in the grammar, not only in the idea."
  },
