   "l": "Go to meet it and you do not see its head;\nfollow after it and you do not see its rear.\nHold fast the Way of antiquity\nand thereby drive what there is today.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Meet it head-on and you see no front to it; follow behind and you see no rear. Take hold of the ancient Way, and with it handle whatever exists now.",
   "n": "御 is the driving of a chariot team — the oldest metaphor a Chinese ruler has for governing, and worth keeping visible in the English. The line before it carries a real manuscript divergence: the received text reads 執古之道, 'hold fast the Way of antiquity', while both Mawangdui silk manuscripts read 執今之道, 'hold fast the Way of today'. One character, and the sentence turns from the authority of the ancient to the sufficiency of the present. We follow the received text, which is Wang Bi's, and the older reading is worth knowing about."
